  <header class="surface-bento page-hero-bento">
    <h1 class="text-page-title">Marine Beach Biotoxins &amp; Freshwater Lake Algae</h1>
    <p class="text-body page-hero-lead">
      Understanding seasonal water quality across King and Snohomish counties: saltwater biotoxins, recreational shellfish closures, freshwater cyanobacteria lake blooms, and pet safety tips.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <h2 class="text-widget-title">Municipal Tap Water Safety &amp; Pet Guidelines</h2>
      <p class="text-body">
        Living near Puget Sound or regional lakes like Lake Washington, Lake Stevens, Green Lake, or Lake Ballinger is one of the best lifestyle perks in Western Washington. First, a quick reassurance: <strong>marine biotoxins and lake algae have zero impact on your home's indoor tap drinking water</strong>, which originates from protected, high-elevation municipal watersheds like the Cedar River or Spada Lake.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body">
        However, for outdoor living, pets, and harvesting shellfish, staying informed is essential. Always follow the pet safety rule for lakes during warm late-summer months: <em>when in doubt, stay out</em>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Freshwater Cyanobacteria (Toxic Blue-Green Algae)</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      During warm, sunny late-summer months, elevated water temperatures combined with nutrient runoff can cause rapid growth of cyanobacteria in local freshwater lakes.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-3">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Visual Identification</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Shoreline Scum</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Toxic algae blooms often look like green pea soup, spilled green paint, or thick scum floating along the shoreline. Wind patterns frequently blow accumulations into swimming coves.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Toxigenic Risks</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Canine Vulnerability</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Cyanobacteria can produce potent nerve toxins (anatoxin-a) or liver toxins (microcystin). Dogs are uniquely at risk because they swallow water while swimming or lick toxic algae scum directly off their fur.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Public Safety</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">State &amp; County Testing</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          The Washington State Department of Ecology and local county health departments routinely collect water samples to test toxicity levels and issue formal swimming beach advisories.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Marine Biotoxins &amp; Shellfish Harvest Rules</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Along Puget Sound saltwater beaches, microscopic marine algae can produce naturally occurring toxins that accumulate in filter-feeding shellfish like clams, oysters, and mussels.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-1">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Neurological Toxin</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Paralytic Shellfish Poison (PSP)</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Associated Illness</span>
            <p class="text-body">Severe neurological illness caused by saxitoxin. Can cause rapid muscle paralysis, tingling in extremities, and respiratory distress.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Safety &amp; Harvest Rule</span>
            <p class="text-body">Cooking or freezing does NOT destroy toxins. Always verify active beach status on the WA Department of Health map before recreational harvesting.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Gastrointestinal Toxin</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Diarrhetic Shellfish Poison (DSP)</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Associated Illness</span>
            <p class="text-body">Acute gastrointestinal illness caused by okadaic acid. Symptoms typically present within hours of consuming contaminated shellfish.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Safety &amp; Harvest Rule</span>
            <p class="text-body">Monitored biweekly by state marine biologists along recreational public beaches during active harvesting seasons.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Warm-Water Bacteria</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Vibrio Parahaemolyticus</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Associated Illness</span>
            <p class="text-body">Naturally occurring marine bacterial infection causing intestinal distress, dehydration, and chills.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Safety &amp; Harvest Rule</span>
            <p class="text-body">Thrives in warm summer mudflats exposed to direct sunlight. Harvest as the tide recedes and place catch on ice immediately.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

